WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.546
Two identical shoes meet two
surfaces: smooth ice and rough

2
00:00:03.546 --> 00:00:04.027
bitumen.

3
00:00:04.027 --> 00:00:06.731
The same sideways push
will act on each shoe.

4
00:00:06.731 --> 00:00:08.474
Which one will slide farther?

5
00:00:08.474 --> 00:00:11.780
Lock in your prediction, then
watch the contact patches

6
00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:13.282
instead of the shoe tops.

7
00:00:13.282 --> 00:00:15.259
The shoe on ice
slips a long way.

8
00:00:15.259 --> 00:00:17.894
The shoe on bitumen
grips and stops quickly.

9
00:00:17.894 --> 00:00:21.248
Between each shoe and surface,
friction acts against the

10
00:00:21.248 --> 00:00:21.787
slipping.

11
00:00:21.787 --> 00:00:25.201
Here, stronger friction helps
the shoe hold its position.

12
00:00:25.201 --> 00:00:29.193
Now the same cart enters tile
and a textured mat at the same

13
00:00:29.193 --> 00:00:29.592
speed.

14
00:00:29.592 --> 00:00:33.118
It rolls farther on tile
and stops sooner on the mat.

15
00:00:33.118 --> 00:00:36.111
This time friction
hinders the cart's motion.

16
00:00:36.111 --> 00:00:39.504
The same force can help
one job and hinder another.

17
00:00:52.725 --> 00:00:55.213
Zoom into the wheel
touching the mat.

18
00:00:55.213 --> 00:00:59.248
The orange arrow is labelled
mat on cart and points opposite

19
00:00:59.248 --> 00:01:01.199
the cart's sliding direction.

20
00:01:01.199 --> 00:01:05.234
Friction is a contact force
between touching surfaces; it is

21
00:01:05.234 --> 00:01:07.924
not a force stored
inside either object.

22
00:01:07.924 --> 00:01:09.062
Compare the jobs.

23
00:01:09.062 --> 00:01:13.147
A runner needs shoe-ground
friction to push without slipping.

24
00:01:13.147 --> 00:01:16.897
A cart designed to coast needs
less friction slowing its

25
00:01:16.897 --> 00:01:17.365
wheels.

26
00:01:17.365 --> 00:01:19.642
Do not label
friction good or bad.

27
00:01:19.642 --> 00:01:23.124
Ask whether it helps or
hinders the intended motion.

28
00:01:23.124 --> 00:01:26.775
Three tile runs stop at 338,
342 and 340 centimetres.

29
00:01:26.775 --> 00:01:30.358
Three mat runs stop at 118,
122 and 120 centimetres.

30
00:01:30.358 --> 00:01:34.492
The shorter repeated distances
show a greater slowing effect

31
00:01:34.492 --> 00:01:36.765
on the mat in this
investigation.

32
00:01:49.985 --> 00:01:53.141
To compare surfaces fairly, keep
the cart, wheels, start line

33
00:01:53.141 --> 00:01:54.434
and entry speed the same.

34
00:01:54.434 --> 00:01:55.676
Change only the surface.

35
00:01:55.676 --> 00:01:58.366
Measure stopping distance
from the entry line, reset

36
00:01:58.366 --> 00:02:00.797
carefully, and repeat
each surface three times.

37
00:02:00.797 --> 00:02:04.278
One trial uses a heavy cart
pushed hard on tile and a light

38
00:02:04.278 --> 00:02:05.989
cart pushed gently on carpet.

39
00:02:05.989 --> 00:02:07.876
That cannot isolate the surface.

40
00:02:07.876 --> 00:02:11.475
More than one condition changed,
so either the cart, the push

41
00:02:11.475 --> 00:02:13.835
or the surface could
explain the result.

42
00:02:13.835 --> 00:02:17.328
A bicycle needs friction at the
brake pads and tyres so it can

43
00:02:17.328 --> 00:02:18.624
slow and grip the road.

44
00:02:18.624 --> 00:02:21.722
It also uses smooth, well-aligned
wheel parts to reduce

45
00:02:21.722 --> 00:02:22.736
unwanted friction.

46
00:02:22.736 --> 00:02:25.835
Good design increases friction
where grip is needed and

47
00:02:25.835 --> 00:02:28.145
reduces it where motion
should stay easy.

48
00:02:41.366 --> 00:02:44.790
Someone claims, friction is
always bad because it wastes

49
00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:45.218
motion.

50
00:02:45.218 --> 00:02:47.174
Test that claim with the runner.

51
00:02:47.174 --> 00:02:50.964
Remove most shoe-ground friction
and the foot slides backward.

52
00:02:50.964 --> 00:02:52.799
The runner cannot push safely.

53
00:02:52.799 --> 00:02:55.244
The evidence does not
support the claim.

54
00:02:55.244 --> 00:02:58.608
Replace the claim with a stronger
one: friction can help or

55
00:02:58.608 --> 00:03:00.261
hinder, depending on the job.

56
00:03:00.261 --> 00:03:02.713
Use evidence, not
surface appearance alone.

57
00:03:02.713 --> 00:03:06.019
Stopping distance shows slowing;
slipping shows lost grip;

58
00:03:06.019 --> 00:03:09.497
repeated fair trials make either
conclusion more trustworthy.

59
00:03:09.497 --> 00:03:13.718
A designer tests two shoe soles
on the same wet path with the

60
00:03:13.718 --> 00:03:15.032
same load and push.

61
00:03:15.032 --> 00:03:18.146
Sole A slips 70 centimetres;
Sole B slips 24.

62
00:03:18.146 --> 00:03:22.366
Which sole gives more useful
friction, and which measurements

63
00:03:22.366 --> 00:03:23.750
justify your choice?
